    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "interesting. so when you say things like drill, mumble rap, or reggaeton have that \"unpolished\" or \"raw\" quality compared to the \"refinement\" of the old money style, are you saying those trends are inherently less sophisticated? just trying to understand if the \"prestige\" you're seeing is more about the actual content of the culture or just the socioeconomic status of the people usually associated with them. like, is there a specific demographic overlap that makes them \"low-brow\" in a marketing sense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'm saying that the \"unpolished\" quality of those trends is subjective, but it's also rooted in the socioeconomic status of the people who typically embody them. Drill, mumble rap, and reggaeton are often associated with urban, low-income communities, which are perceived as being less refined and less cultured. This perception is perpetuated by the media and marketing industries, which tend to focus on the most visible and attention-grabbing aspects of these cultures.\n\nIn marketing terms, the \"prestige\" of old money and preppy aesthetics is often tied to the idea of exclusivity and high social status. These styles are often associated with affluent, educated, and influential individuals who value tradition, heritage, and refinement. In contrast, the trends I listed earlier are often seen as more \"authentic\" and \"raw\" because they're associated with marginalized communities that are perceived as being less powerful and less influential.\n\nHowever, it's worth noting that this perception is not necessarily objective. The \"prestige\" of certain styles is often a social construct, created and maintained by the dominant culture to distinguish itself from the perceived \"other.\" By marketing certain styles as more refined and sophisticated, luxury brands are, in effect, perpet",
      "source": "target",
